=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation found no fault in the inter-VLAN routing path between VLAN 10 and VLAN 30 on mswitch0. All three of the multilayer switch's SVIs — vlan10@br0 (172.16.10.254/24), vlan20@br0 (172.16.20.254/24), and vlan30@br0 (172.16.30.254/24) — are fully UP and properly addressed. Linux IP forwarding is enabled (ip_forward=1) on mswitch0, and the routing table carries directly-connected /24 routes for all three campus VLANs.

The bridge VLAN table on mswitch0 does show only VLAN 1 on its trunk ports (eth2, eth3, eth4), which could appear suspicious, but this is irrelevant: `vlan_filtering` is explicitly **disabled** (`/sys/class/net/br0/bridge/vlan_filtering = 0`), so those VLAN database entries carry no enforcement — all tagged and untagged frames traverse the bridge unconditionally and reach the correct SVI via the Linux 802.1Q sub-interface mechanism.

Live traffic tests confirm complete connectivity. PC5 (172.16.10.5, VLAN 10) successfully pinged every tested host on VLAN 30 (172.16.30.12, .13, .14) with 0% packet loss and TTL=63, confirming exactly one routing hop through mswitch0. The reverse direction (VLAN 30 → VLAN 10) was equally healthy. The upstream access switches (switch1 sending tagged VLAN 10, switch3 sending tagged VLAN 30) are correctly trunking to mswitch0.

Root cause: There is no fault. The helpdesk ticket premise — that an SVI is down or misconfigured — is incorrect. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===